WEBVTT

NOTE Module 10 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (136.2s). en.

1
00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:02.902
Welcome to Module Ten — production deployment.

2
00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:11.697
This is the last mile: getting a trained model out of the notebook and in front of real users.

3
00:00:12.063 --> 00:00:15.734
We'll cover quantization, TFLite, TensorFlow Serving, TFX pipelines, and monitoring.

4
00:00:16.084 --> 00:00:20.186
Start by making the model smaller and faster with quantization.

5
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.169
Post-training dynamic quantization shrinks the weights.

6
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:28.636
Full integer quantization takes activations down to int-eight using a small calibration set.

7
00:00:29.024 --> 00:00:31.736
Float-sixteen simply halves the size with no calibration.

8
00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:43.249
And when accuracy matters most, quantization-aware training fine-tunes with fake quantization in the loop — expect roughly two-to-four times faster inference and about four times smaller.

9
00:00:44.257 --> 00:00:46.850
For phones and edge devices, that's TensorFlow Lite.

10
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:58.526
Convert from a Keras model, run it through the Interpreter on Android, iOS, or even microcontrollers, and lean on delegates — GPU, NNAPI, Hexagon — to accelerate.

11
00:00:58.913 --> 00:01:03.567
Always verify accuracy on the target hardware, because some ops may be unsupported.

12
00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:07.130
On the server side, TensorFlow Serving takes over.

13
00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:20.478
Export to the SavedModel format, lay it out as model name then version number, and serve REST on port eighty-five-oh-one or gRPC on eighty-five-hundred — gRPC being roughly twice as fast.

14
00:01:20.854 --> 00:01:27.707
A few warmup requests prevent cold-start latency spikes, and the official Docker image makes deployment routine.

15
00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:42.647
To make the whole thing reproducible, TFX strings it into a pipeline: ingest, validate statistics and schema, train, evaluate, and push to serving — where schema drift blocks bad data before it ever reaches production.

16
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.249
Orchestrate it with Kubeflow, Airflow, or Vertex AI.

17
00:01:46.622 --> 00:01:49.828
And the job isn't done at deploy.

18
00:01:50.181 --> 00:01:56.884
Watch for data drift as inputs shift and concept drift as the input-to-output relationship changes.

19
00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:09.332
Run a KS test or PSI on your features regularly, roll out with shadow and canary deploys, and log predictions against eventual ground truth so you can retrain.

20
00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:13.843
Let's ship your model — open the labs.

21
00:02:14.212 --> 00:02:15.689
And congratulations: that's the course.
